ng mandate. So applicability 4.\n\nNovelty originality: degree rare but ingenious... Is packaging compliance as market differentiator common? Probably unusual interesting. Not expressed before? Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Could be 2 = interesting. Probably 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves/modifies paradigm. 4 = paradigm breaking introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This proposal changes relationship? Turns legal obligation into procurement-ready sales document, partners compliance with marketing. That's changing relationship, maybe paradigm stretching not breaking? It still treats compliance as logging practice but adds market differentiation. Might be 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Yes partners compliance office with product marketing to produce certification brief. New offering. So 3.\n\nWorkability acceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms? Marketing audit-ready is acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nImplementability: degree easily implemented. Audit existing systems against fields, produce certification brief per system, partner compliance with marketing. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? Requires audit and documentation. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Probably 3.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nProvide output with id P2516 and fields.\n\nFormat: probably list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nP2516\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure order: effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, acceptability, implementability\n\nReturn rating echoing id exactly.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Auditing systems against Article 12(3) fields and creating certification briefs will document compliance but does not itself build the automatic logging infrastructure required by Article 12(1)-(2).\n\nScore 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets meeting Article 12 logging minimums for Annex III 1(a) systems and mapping logs to traceability requirements.\n\nScore 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Turning mandatory logging into an auditable market asset with compliance-marketing co-produced certification briefs is unusual for R&D labs facing the AI Act.\n\nScore 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It stretches the paradigm by making compliance a product differentiator and creating new marketing-compliance partnership rather than treating logging as internal overhead.\n\nScore 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Promoting audit-ready systems and transparent traceability mapping aligns with legal expectations and procurement norms in regulated sectors.\n\nScore 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Auditing existing/in-development systems and producing per-system briefs requires cross-functional work but uses existing artifacts and does not demand fundamental R&D redesign.\n\nScore 3.\n\nOutput.",
