ity (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, reasonably feasible\nSchema linter plus replay test runs on every merge. That's some engineering effort but feasible. Could be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not trivially easy. I'd say 3.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nIt ensures logs exist before production. It addresses logging mandate. Might solve main problem of meeting logging mandate? It ensures completeness gate but does not guarantee correctness of content, post-market monitoring etc. It contributes. Probably 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial solution. Could be 4? It solves logging mandate for production promotion. Problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Gate ensures compliance before promotion. Might solve main problem without regard workability. Hmm.\n\nThe problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources. This gate would ensure compliance. I think effectiveness 3? Could be 4. \"reasonable and will solve the stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\". If you could implement perfectly, would it solve logging mandate? It ensures logs emitted, stored, queryable. Might still miss Article 12 specific event classes like risk situations etc. But gate checks every Article 12 event class. Could be sufficient.\n\nMaybe effectiveness = 3 (contributes). I'll give 3.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nThis directly addresses meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer).\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give ... first and then ... Could output lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1324\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure evidence is one sentence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The CI/CD log-completeness gate is an unusual but recognizable shift-left compliance check for AI logging, not a completely novel invention. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces linter and replay tests into development but still treats compliance as logging practice rather than redefining R&D-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Blocking promotion until logs are complete is a common engineering quality gate that violates no legal or social norms. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building schema conformance linter and synthetic replay tests requires non-trivial engineering work but is reasonably feasible within a typical lab CI/CD pipeline. Score 3.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The gate would catch missing Article 12 log classes early and contribute substantially to meeting the logging mandate, though it does not by itself guarantee ongoing post-market traceability. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly addresses how the lab can meet Article 12 automatic recording and traceability requirements before production release. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
