hing: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal co-designs model topology and logging infrastructure simultaneously, positions lab at frontier. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations: logging woven into science. That's paradigm stretching? Might be paradigm breaking? It introduces new elements AND changes relationship... Could be 4. It addresses larger problem of what lab researches? Maybe 4.\n\nThe description: \"introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between the lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses the larger problem of what the lab researches\". It does change relationship: logging as first-class primitive. Might be paradigm breaking. I'd pick 4? Hmm slightly stretching might be 3. Let's choose 4 for paradigm breaking.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\nThis is research, acceptable. Might be 4? Common strategies? Research programme is common. Unusual but acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Or 3? It's somewhat uncommon. I'd say 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nMulti-year project developing neural-system architectures... benchmarking across... That's significant change, expensive/difficult. So implementability = 2? Significant change, expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Could be 2.\n\nThus ratings:\neffectiveness evidence sentence then score\napplicability...\noriginality...\nparadigm_relatedness...\nacceptability...\nimplementability...\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Probably list. The instruction says return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\".\n\nI'll produce structured output.\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences:\n\neffectiveness_evidence: The programme aims for long-term architectural research rather than delivering immediate log records required by Articles 12 and Recital 71. -> score 3? Wait if it's long-term, maybe effectiveness 2. Hmm.\n\nAlternative reasoning: If you could do it, would solve main problem? It would eventually reduce logging overhead but not meet mandate now. Maybe effectiveness 3 = contributes partially.\n\nI'll go with effectiveness 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem because multi-year research doesn't meet current mandate. Actually definition 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem (it probably will not work). Might fit.\n\nLet's decide: The lab needs to meet logging mandate now; a multi-year architecture research programme does not provide compliant logs in time. So effectiveness = 2.\n\napplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets automatic event logging for high-risk AI systems as mandated by Article 12. -> score 4\n\noriginality evidence: Co-designing model topology with logging as a first-class primitive is unusual and imaginative for an R&D lab response to compliance. -> score 3\n\nparadigm_relatedness evidence: It reframes compliance from bolt-on logging to a core architectural research agenda, changing the relationship between R&D and regulatory obligations. -> score 4\n\nacceptability evidence: Developing observable AI architectures and publishing trade-off results is socially and legally unobjectionable within an EU-regulated lab. -> score 4\n\nimplementability evid