agination.\n\nEvidence: The idea proposes a quantified conformance harness with pass-rate metrics rather than ad-hoc checklist reviews.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem related...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = ...\n\nProblem: must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Proposal addresses meeting logging mandate by ensuring prototype logging captures required event types. Directly related. So 4.\n\nEvidence: It directly targets whether prototypes technically allow automatic recording of Article 12(2)(a)-(c) and Annex III minimums.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but partial)\nWill logging fidelity benchmark solve meeting logging mandate? It catches gaps early, but doesn't itself implement logs. It helps ensure compliance but does not guarantee logs exist. Might be partial. Could be 3 = contributes. Might be 4 if you could do it, it would solve main problem of viability risks surfacing early. Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing resources to pursue proposal. The proposal gives objective metric for whether system technically allows automatic recording so viability risks surface early rather than at integration/audit. That is partial solution, not full implementation. So effectiveness 3? Could be 4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It would not actually create logs, just test them. Might be partial.\n\nI'll rate 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nEvidence: It improves detection of logging gaps early but does not