        "content": "{\n  \"ratings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"P1857\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness_evidence\": \"The proposal merges regulatory documentation with open-science publication norms, making compliance feed scientific output rather than remain separate overhead.\",\n      \"paradigm_relatedness\": 3,\n      \"originality_evidence\": \"Pairing Recital 71 documentation requirements with ML venue disclosure norms is uncommon in typical lab compliance plans.\",\n      \"originality\": 3,\n      \"effectiveness_evidence\": \"It targets technical documentation under Recital 71 rather than the automatic event logging required by Article 12, so it does not solve the logging mandate.\",\n      \"effectiveness\": 1,\n      \"applicability_evidence\": \"Documentation templates help general compliance but do not provide the automatic logs Article 12 requires for high-risk systems.\",\n      \"applicability\": 2,\n      \"acceptability_evidence\": \"Publishing model cards and dataset provenance aligns with academic norms and EU transparency expectations.\",\n      \"acceptability\": 4,\n      \"implementability_evidence\": \"Mapping fields and piloting templates requires cross-team coordination but uses existing publication workflows.\",\n      \"implementability\": 3\n    }\n  ]\n}",
        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nProposal P1857: Compliance Documentation as Open-Science Contribution. Develop standardized technical-documentation templates that satisfy Recital 71... while simultaneously meeting disclosure norms of top-tier ML venues. Work with publications team to map each compliance field onto sections reviewers expect, then pilot merged template on two active high-risk projects before institutionalizing lab-wide. This raises intellectual merit and citation impact while ensuring no research milestone goes undocumented.\n\nWe need rate six subscales.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal merges compliance documentation with open-science publication. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations: compliance becomes publication asset. Is that paradigm breaking? It introduces new elements AND changes relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science, new partners/offers born from compliance). It doesn't seem to change what lab researches, just how docs are used. So 3 = paradigm stretching.\n\nEvidence: merges regulatory documentation with publication norms, making compliance feed scientific output rather than remain separate