tone goes undocumented for regulatory purposes. That's relevant to Recital 71, not Article 12 logs. The problem explicitly mentions logging mandate. Might be considered solves unrelated problem => 1.\n\nBut applicability might be lower.\n\nLet's decide effectiveness = 2? unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem (it probably will not work). Actually documentation templates won't create logs.\n\nThe anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. 2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem. 1 = solves unrelated problem.\n\nI think it solves an unrelated problem (documentation vs logging). So 1.\n\nEvidence sentence: It targets technical documentation under Recital 71 rather than automatic event logging required by Article 12.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention is not stated or does not produce useful outcome\n\nDoes it clearly apply? It applies to compliance documentation, which is part of overall AI Act but not the logging mandate. Might be 2: may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates to stated problem. Or 3 solves implied problem related (ensuring traceability). Hmm.\n\nI think applicability = 3? Solves implied problem that is related: documentation supports traceability needed for logging compliance? Could be 2.\n\nLet's pick applicability 2: may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates to stated problem. Evidence: Documentation templates help general compliance but do not provide the automatic logs Article 12 requires for high-risk systems.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 = radically violates laws...\n\nOpen-science contribution is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Or 3? It's somewhat uncommon to merge compliance with publications but still acceptable. I'd say 4.\n\nEvidence: Publishing model cards and dataset provenance aligns with academic norms and EU transparency expectations.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDevelop standardized templates, work with publications team, pilot on two projects then institutionalize. That's reasonably feasible. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Requires coordination but low cost. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: Mapping fields and piloting templates requires cross-team coordination but uses existing publication workflows.\n\nReturn JSON? Format requested: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably output lines.\n\nWe'll output id P1857 then evidence sentences and scores.\n\nLet's produce structured.\n\n",
