retching changing relationship? Might be 3? \"logging woven into the science\" is example of paradigm stretching. Hmm embedding in standard dev environment so every prototype captures... That is weaving logging into science? Probably slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nI'd say paradigm_relatedness = 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious imaginative surprising\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nUnified logging middleware for compliance is fairly common practice. Shared logging layer in dev environment is common. Might be common, mundane. Could be 1? Or 2 interesting? Probably common. I'd rate 1 = common mundane boring.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\nProposal does exactly logging mandate: captures start-end timestamps, reference DB, matched input data, verifier identities. That's directly Article 12(3). So applicability 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nIdea will capture required logs for new prototypes. It doesn't address legacy systems audit gaps? Says while we audit legacy... Might partially solve. But for future projects, yes solves. Effectiveness 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem (logging mandate) for new prototypes. Might be partial because legacy not covered but still helps. Probably 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard workability. Might be 3 contributes? Hmm \"solves an identified problem that is directly related\". The problem is must decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. This solves for new prototypes. I'd say 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be