on. Not necessarily never expressed before.\n\nApplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nProblem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal meets logging mandate via using logs for research. Does it solve mandate? It uses logs already generated. Applicability 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related: need to decide how to respond to mandate. It reframes compliance as research. Might be 4.\n\nEffectiveness: degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\nProposal converts cost into output, ensures regulation funds new research rather than merely absorbing budget. Does it solve logging mandate? It assumes logs already exist. Might contribute but not ensure compliance itself. Maybe effectiveness 3 = contributes to solution (makes compliance less burdensome). Could be 4.\n\nLet's decide: applicability evidence: proposal directly addresses how lab responds to Article 12 logging mandate by mining required logs. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: mining logs will not by itself guarantee compliant logging but will make compliance productive and support post-market monitoring under Article 72, contributing to solution. Might be 3.\n\nWe need one sentence evidence for each subscale then score.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? The instruction says return rating... For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could be plain text lines.\n\nI'll produce structured list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2900\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nProceed.\n\n",
