l solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute...\nIdea is funding logs early. Would that solve logging mandate? It ensures resources exist, but still need technical implementation. Might be partial solution: ensures compliance readiness but does not itself build logs. Probably contributes to solution, not fully solve. The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Funding gates ensure adaptation. Might be reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial). Could argue if you could do it, it would solve main problem? It prevents scramble but still need actual logging tech. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Embedding traceability budgets and checkpoints makes compliance resourced, though it does not itself build the automatic logs required by Article 12.\n\nScore 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nThis is internal funding reallocation, mandatory traceability budget. Seems common, does not violate norms. Maybe somewhat uncommon? Adding 8% budget is reasonable. I'd say 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEvidence: Internal grant restructuring and compliance budgeting are routine administrative practices with no legal or ethical objections.\n\nScore 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\nRestructure project-grant and milestone-review process, work with grants office to add mandatory budget reviewed quarterly. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Not easy low cost? Requires organizational change. Might be 3.\n\nEvidence: It requires coordination with the grants office and process changes but does not demand new technology or regulatory approval.\n