pt R&D accordingly. Adapting R&D accordingly could be reframing obligation as research opportunity. That is adapting R&D. So it contributes to solution of problem? Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt its R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nSo effectiveness about solving problem of meeting mandate / adapting R&D. The proposal does not implement logs; it's a theoretical sub-project. Might contribute to adaptation but not solve logging mandate. Could be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution. I'd say it contributes partially to adaptation but doesn't meet logging mandate. Could be 2 unreasonable or unlikely to solve problem because resources committed to theoretical research won't meet logging requirement.\n\nMaybe effectiveness = 2 (unreasonable or unlikely to solve the problem). Because meeting mandate requires technical logging implementation, not formal model.\n\nLet's choose effectiveness 2? Or 3? The definition: will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but it is only a partial solution). Does formal model help meet logging mandate? Could inform design. Possibly partial.\n\nI think rating as 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps adapt R&D). But does it solve logging mandate? Hmm.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly relates to mandated logging constraints. So applicability likely 4? It solves identified problem that is directly related: traceability/logging. Or is it implied? It's about interpretability guarantees from logging. That's directly related to Article 12. Might be 4.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not