ship: logging as first-order design constraint, publishable engineering science. Might be paradigm breaking? Perhaps stretching is more accurate: logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance. The description matches 3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science...). Could be 4 because it addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Hmm.\n\nI think 3 is safe: paradigm stretching.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly.\nProposal directly addresses meeting logging mandate via design principle, measuring costs. Solves identified problem directly related. So 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability? It builds prototypes with different granularity, releases design guide. Will that solve logging mandate? It contributes to solution, but maybe partial? It explores minimal-yet-sufficient logs, measures costs. Might solve main problem of how to meet logging mandate? Could be 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Actually it produces design guide, not necessarily implement in lab's own systems. But proposal is research programme treating logging as design constraint. Might be partial. I think effectiveness is 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Because it's research programme, not immediate compliance. Could still solve? Hmm.\n\nWorkability acceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms... It's research on logging, open design guide. Acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Building three prototype high-risk systems with controlled ablation studies measuring computational and privacy costs, releasing results as open design guide. That's significant change, expensive or difficult. So 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Or 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Building three prototypes is major resource commitment. Probably 2.\n\nLet's decide scores:\noriginality: 3\nparadigm_relatedness: 3 (or 4). Let's pick 3 to be conservative.\napplicability: 4\neffectiveness: 3\nacceptability: 4\nimplementability: 2\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating before score.\n\nProvide output with id and fields.\n\nFormat: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating. Might be lines.\n\nI'll output structured.\n\n",
