ied problem that is directly related.\n\nEvidence: The proposal targets Article 12 and Recital 71 record-keeping requirements by using existing evaluation outputs as compliance logs.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common.\n\nUsing evaluation pipelines as compliance logs seems plausible but not extremely surprising? Might be unusual interesting. Could be paradigm stretching. I'd say originality = 3? Common practice of dual-use documentation is somewhat common. Hmm. Maybe 2 = interesting. Let's think: reference class is set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give. Many labs would propose building logging infrastructure separate. Re-purposing evaluation artefacts is somewhat unusual but not ingenious. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. I'll go with 3.\n\nEvidence: Repurposing publication-grade benchmark artefacts as living compliance evidence is an imaginative dual-use design rather than a standard add-on logger.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nThis proposal weaves logging into science, makes evaluation SOP generate compliance. That's paradigm stretching: changes relationship. Not breaking? Might be stretching. Description says logging woven into the science, new partners... So 3.\n\nEvidence: It embeds compliance generation inside the scientific validation workflow 