ing how to respond. Might be 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial)\nStudy gives hard numbers before lock in design. It contributes to solution but does not itself implement logging mandate. It informs decision. So effectiveness 3? Could be 4? Will solve stated problem? Stated problem is must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Study provides information to decide, not actually meet mandate. So partial solution. So 3.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nFeasibility study is essentially standard due diligence. It introduces measurement but treats compliance as logging practice. Probably paradigm preserving? Or slightly paradigm stretching? Introduces new elements (load test with Article 12(3)-conformant schemas). But still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 1 = standard compliance and logging practice. Hmm.\n\nThe study is a feasibility study before design freeze. That's common engineering practice. Probably paradigm preserving. It doesn't change relationship bet