        "reasoning_content": "You are a rater applying idea-evaluation instrument... Apply published anchored levels exactly, one level per subscale, and give one sentence of evidence for each rating.\n\nWe need rate proposal P2653.\n\nProposal: Draft Log Retention and Access Policy for EU Deployments. Define institutional rules governing how long system logs stored, who may access them, how preserved across software updates or decommissioning. Compliance office drafts binding internal policy specifying retention periods aligned to AI system lifetime, encryption at rest, role-based access controls, step-by-step procedures for producing logs on demand under Article 72 post-market monitoring obligations. Clear governance prevents conflicts with GDPR data-minimization principles while ensuring lab can produce complete unaltered records when notified body requests.\n\nRate subscales:\n\napplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work.\n\nProposal is about drafting log retention and access policy. Does it apply? It addresses logging mandate, retention, access, preservation across updates. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, traceability etc. Recital 71 mentions technical documentation kept up to date throughout lifetime, logs. So retention/access policy applies directly.\n\nLevel 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Drafting policy to meet logging mandate is directly related. So applicability =4.\n\nEvidence sentence: The proposal creates governance for log storage and access required to satisfy Article 12 lifetime logging and Article 72 post-market monitoring requests.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem)\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\nPolicy helps but does not technically enable automatic logging; it's governance around logs. It contributes but maybe not solve technical logging mandate? The mandate is technically allow automatic recording. Policy addresses retention/access, not recording capability itself. Might be partial solution.\n\nDoes it solve stated problem of how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D? It ensures compliance with record-keeping obligations post generation. But Article 12(1) requires technically allow automatic recording. Policy doesn't ensure technical logging exists. So effectiveness maybe 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial).\n\nEvidence: A retention and access policy ensures logs can be produced intact for authorities but does not create the automatic logging capability Article 12 requires.\n\nScore 3.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nDrafting internal policy is standard compliance practice. Paradigm preserving? Might be slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces governance but still treats compliance as logging practice. Probably paradigm preserving: standard compliance.\n\nThe anchored level 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Policy drafting is standard.\n\nSo paradigm_relatedness =1.\n\nEvidence: Drafting a retention/access policy is conventional compliance administration that leaves R&D workflows and the lab's relationship to compliance unchanged.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nDraft log retention policy is common, mundane. So 1.\n\nEvidence: Institutional log retention and access policies are a common, mundane response to regulated record-keeping mandates.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible...\n\nDrafting policy is easy, low cost. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Writing an internal policy with defined retention, encryption and access controls requires compliance office drafting rather than engineering overhaul.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nPolicy is acceptable. So 4.\n\nEvidence: Formal data governance aligned with GDPR minimization and AI Act inspection rights is socially, legally and politically acceptable.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nNeed output format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating... give ... Might be plain text. Safer to provide structured lines.\n\nProbably output:\nid: P2653\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nLet's produce clearly.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence each.\n\nCheck order: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide each.\n\n",
