ents (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nPublishing white paper converts compliance into sales asset. That changes relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. Might be paradigm stretching? Changing relationship = 3. Paradigm breaking requires addresses larger problem of what lab researches. Maybe not.\n\nSo paradigm_relatedness = 3?\n\nOriginality: 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination 2 = interesting 1 = common mundane\n\nCustomer-facing traceability white paper is perhaps unusual but not super ingenious. Might be 3? Or 2? I'd say 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's produce output with id P1611 and subscale_evidence and subscale.\n\nFormat: maybe JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\". Probably output lines.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P1611\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 2\n...\n\nMake sure order: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nLet's craft evidence sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The white paper describes existing logs but does not create automatic event recording or logging capabilities required by Article 12(1)-(3). Score 2.\n\nApplicability evidence: It addresses procurement transparency rather than the technical logging mandate, relating only indirectly to meeting Article 12. Score 2? Might be 3. Hmm decide 3? Let's keep 2.\n\nImplementability evidence: Creating and quarterly refreshing a public document with marketing-engineering co-authorship is reasonably feasible with modest staff time. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Proactive transparency about traceability is socially and legally unobjectionable and aligns with regulatory expectations. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It refr