ce adapted to AI Act fields rather than a novel inventive approach.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nProposal introduces CI test suite for logs. Still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces new tools/processes. So slightly paradigm stretching: 2.\n\nEvidence: It adds a tooling layer to prototyping but keeps compliance framed as correct log output rather than rethinking R&D or compliance relationship.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. This is normal testing. Acceptability 4.\n\nEvidence: Building automated checks into CI to enforce regulatory log fields is a routine engineering practice that violates no legal, social or business norms.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes? Need parameterised test scripts per risk category, integrate Jenkins, staging inference endpoint. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 easy? Needs dev effort. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: It requires writing parameterized tests and wiring them into existing Jenkins and a staging endpoint, which is feasible but involves non-trivial engineering effort beyond trivial configuration.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably as JSON? Instructio