ree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\nLevels: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal converts regulatory obligation into design constraint, architecture review before testing. It introduces reusable SDK, middleware. Does it change relationship? It weaves logging into development pipeline, making compliance a design constraint. That's paradigm stretching? Slightly paradigm stretching maybe: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. It says converts obligation into design constraint - that changes relationship? Might be 3 = changes that relationship (logging woven into science). Hmm.\n\nThe description: \"This converts a regulatory obligation into a design constraint\". That sounds like changing relationship between R&D and compliance obligations. That's paradigm stretching per definition: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science...). Could be 3. Slightly paradigm stretching is introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. They do treat as design constraint, which is more than just logging practice.\n\nI'd rate 3. Evidence: It embeds logging as a mandatory architectural gate rather than an after-the-fact add-on.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nEmbedding middleware logging SDK for compliance is