sonable and will contribute...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely\n1 = solves unrelated\n\nMandatory logging layer captures events relevant to risk identification, substantial modifications, operational monitoring. Article 12 requires automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, enabling recording of events relevant... So this directly addresses mandate. Would solve problem. Probably 4.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied...\nProposal is do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem. Yes directly related. Score 4.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nProposal is standardized non-bypassable logging module embedded at architecture level... This introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's slightly paradigm stretching? It says embed at architecture level before leaves lab. Could be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2.\n\nAlternatively could be 1 = standard compliance and logging practice; lab way unchanged? But embedding SDK into standard dev framework is change. I'd say 2.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nMandatory logging layer in all high-risk pipelines is common compliance response. Common