e paradigm stretching because logging woven into science? Hmm.\n\nThe description: \"changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\". This gate makes logging part of design phase. Could be considered changes relationship. However slightly paradigm stretching says introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Might be 2.\n\nI think it's 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Evidence sentence.\n\noriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nA mandatory checkpoint for logging architecture is fairly common in regulated dev (gate review). Might be common, mundane. Probably 1 or 2. It's a standard compliance gate. I'd say common, mundane, boring = 1.\n\nBut could be interesting? Pre-build logging architecture gate is somewhat typical. Likely common. Score 1.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\n3 = solves implied problem...\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. Applies to stated problem. So 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\nWill a gate ensure compliance with Articles 12? It ensures spec exists before coding, front-loads review. Might contribute but doesn't guarantee logs actually implemented correctly or maintained. It helps solve problem of meeting mandate, avoids re-engineering. Could be 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability. If you could do it, would solve main problem? It ensures logging architecture is reviewed before coding, which should ensure compliance. Maybe partial. I'd say 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but only partial). Because spec approval doesn't guarantee runtime logging.\n\nHmm effectiveness rating: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem is decide how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Gate ensures specs reviewed. Probably solves. Might be 4.\n\nLet's choose 3? Let's think anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). If gate enforced, then compliance should be met. Could still have implementation drift. But spec approval before coding seems sufficient to ensure logging capability designed. I'd say 3 is safer: contributes but partial.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nAdding gate to existing quarterly reviews, small panel. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nGate review is common, acceptable. Score 4.\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentences and scores.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably as lines.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably plain.\n\nWill echo id exactly.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P2253\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\netc.\n\nMake one sentence evidence each.\n\nEnsure one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
